    <section class="pricing-detail" id="pricing"><div class="container">
        <h2>What will the installation cost?</h2>
        <p>The wiring route usually makes a big difference. A charger beside the panel takes less work than one across the house or in a detached garage. We'll also check how much electrical capacity you have and whether you already own a charger.</p>
        <p>You don't need to figure all that out first. Tell us what you know. We'll follow up and arrange a walkthrough if we need a closer look.</p>
        <a class="button dark" href="/get-help.html?service=ev">Start your installation request →</a>
    </div></section>
    <section class="content-section"><div class="container"><div class="content-wrapper">
        <h2>Choose the charging speed you need</h2>
        <p>A bigger charger won't make every car charge faster. Your vehicle sets its own limit, and your daily mileage matters more than the largest number on the box.</p>
        <div class="content-columns">
            <div><h3>Level 1: a regular outlet</h3><p>Level 1 uses 120 volts. It can suit a plug-in hybrid or a driver who covers fewer miles and has plenty of time to charge. The outlet and circuit still need to suit the charger; don't assume an old garage outlet is ready for it.</p></div>
            <div><h3>Level 2: faster home charging</h3><p>Level 2 uses a 240-volt circuit. Many drivers choose it so they can put back the day's driving overnight. Charging time depends on your vehicle, charger settings, and how much charge you need.</p></div>
        </div>
        <h3>Tell us the model and year</h3>
        <p>Charging limits can change between model years and trims. We check the actual vehicle and charger before sizing the circuit. That applies whether you drive a Tesla, Ford, Chevy, Rivian, or another EV.</p>
        <p>If you plan to add a second EV later, mention it now. We can compare the cost of preparing for it with what you need today.</p>

        <h2>Hardwired charger or plug-in charger?</h2>
        <p>A hardwired charger connects directly to the circuit. A plug-in charger uses a matching outlet. Either can work well when the equipment and installation suit the location.</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Already have a charger?</strong> Give us the model so we can check its installation requirements.</li>
            <li><strong>Park outside?</strong> We use equipment and connections rated for outdoor use.</li>
            <li><strong>Want a plug-in setup?</strong> We check the outlet type, circuit, and required protection. A 240-volt outlet isn't automatically the right one.</li>
            <li><strong>Still choosing?</strong> We can talk through cable reach, connector compatibility, scheduling, and load management before you buy.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>We install home charging equipment including Tesla Wall Connectors, ChargePoint, and Wallbox. The right choice depends on your car and home.</p>

        <h2>Will you need a panel upgrade?</h2>
        <p>Not necessarily. We calculate the power your home needs before recommending an upgrade. The panel's amp rating and the number of empty breaker spaces don't tell the whole story.</p>
        <p>A compatible load management system can reduce charging while other appliances use more power. That can sometimes let you add a charger without increasing your electrical service.</p>
        <p>For two EVs, we can look at compatible chargers that share available power. We'll check the manufacturer's requirements for the exact setup.</p>
        <p><a href="/electrical-panel-upgrade.html">Read about panel upgrades and the alternatives →</a></p>

        <h2>What happens during installation?</h2>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>We review the job.</strong> Your vehicle, parking spot, panel location, and plans for future charging help us work out the scope.</li>
            <li><strong>We explain the work and price.</strong> We identify the wiring route, any electrical changes, and what the estimate includes.</li>
            <li><strong>We install and check the charger.</strong> We handle the electrical permit and inspection arrangements and show you how to use the setup.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>A finished basement, detached garage, long wiring route, or underground work can add time. Tell us about those when you send your request.</p>

        <h2>Check DTE's current programs</h2>
        <p>DTE offers EV rate options and a home charger rebate with eligibility requirements, including household income. Check the current terms before counting a rebate toward your budget. Your rate plan determines the cheaper charging hours.</p>
        <p><a href="https://dteenergy.com/us/en/residential/service-request/pev/home-ev-charger-rebate.html">Check DTE's home charger rebate →</a></p>

        <h2>Common questions</h2>
        <h3>Should I install a larger circuit for my next car?</h3>
        <p>Tell us what you might buy next. We can compare preparing the wiring now with keeping this installation smaller. A larger circuit only helps if your home and charging equipment can use it.</p>
        <h3>Can I use the dryer outlet?</h3>
        <p>Ask us to check it first. The outlet, wiring, circuit rating, and charger must match. Sharing a circuit may need approved load management equipment. Don't assume a plug adapter makes it suitable.</p>
        <h3>Can you install a charger in a detached garage?</h3>
        <p>Yes. We check the power already available in the garage and the route for any new wiring. Underground work can affect the price.</p>
        <h3>Do I need to buy the charger first?</h3>
        <p>No. Tell us about your car and where you park. We can help you choose before you buy, or install suitable equipment you already own.</p>
    </div></div></section>
